TITLE:: ParaEQPanel
summary:: an eq's controls as one view: header, response, meters, the cards in frequency order
categories:: Libraries>ParaEQ
related:: Classes/ParaEQ, Classes/ParaEQGUI, Classes/ParaEQView, Classes/ParaEQSpectrum, Classes/FxHintBar

DESCRIPTION::
What every window that shows an eq is built from: the eq's own window, the
compressor's sidechain page, the master chain in ServerPanel, the module family's
eq tab. The frame every effect window shares - the header, a display with the
meters at its right, titled cards, the knobs and their gestures - with an
equaliser's face. From the top: the header (power, the name in ivory, the presets, save, the
code::...:: menu, then the display's height and the spectrum switch); the response
with the meters at its right; three cards - the low cut, the band shown with its
1 2 3 4 5 selector, the high cut.

strong::One plane you play.:: The link::Classes/ParaEQView:: is the instrument:
drag a dot, wheel on it, double click it. Every gesture is there.

strong::The cards read low to high,:: under the axis they work on: the low cut, the
band shown, the high cut. One band at a time, the way a channel strip shows its
selected band - click a dot or a number in the selector. Each band's colour is the
same on its dot, its fill, its number and its knobs' arcs; a bell's width reads in
octaves, a shelf's in percent (100 % is the steepest shelf with no bump at its
corner).

strong::The cuts are switches with slopes.:: code::in:: on the title line puts one
in; out, as every builtin preset but code::radio::, code::'kick weight'::
and code::air:: leaves them, its knobs dim and its dot is a ring - it does nothing
and costs nothing. code::res:: is how the corner rings: 0 the flat Butterworth
corner, up rings, down rounds it off; it dims at 6 dB/oct, where there is no corner
to ring. A band at 0 dB is the identity filter, so its frequency and width dim
until its gain moves.

strong::No mix, no output.:: An eq mixed with its own dry signal combs, which is why
equalisers have no mix; and ParaEQ adds no gain stage of its own, so the band card
takes that width.

The meters at the right are the levels going in and out of the eq, read from its
node while the panel is running - see link::Classes/ParaEQ#-meter::. A panel on an
eq with nothing to read them from (a holder of settings, as the compressor's
sidechain eq is) leaves them out.

Typing a frequency takes Hz (code::2k::, code::350::) or a Tidal note
(code::e2:: is 41.2 Hz, code::a5:: 440). The header's code::...:: menu holds save
as, delete, copy, paste, back to flat and the hints; at its right, the display's
height and the spectrum switch, one for every eq window.

It follows one link::Classes/ParaEQ:: and can be pointed at another, which is how a
module window shows the eq of whichever pad is selected:

code::
(
e = ParaEQ.new.preset_(\warm);
w = Window("eq", Rect(100, 100, 960, 520)).front;
p = ParaEQPanel(e);
h = FxHintBar();
w.layout = VLayout(p.view, h.view);
h.watch(w);
p.hints = h;                  // labels every control
p.running = true;             // the analysis and the meters, while it is showing
w.onClose = { p.remove };
);
p.eq = ParaEQ.new.preset_(\loudness);
::

CLASSMETHODS::

METHOD:: new
ARGUMENT:: eq
ARGUMENT:: parent
ARGUMENT:: subject
What the hints call the thing being shaped - "this pad", "the sidechain".
ARGUMENT:: showOnBox
code::false:: leaves the power button out, for a window that has one of its own.
ARGUMENT:: compact
code::true:: for a host short of height: the band's knobs medium rather than big,
and a lower display. About 270 px tall at the least, against 336.

INSTANCEMETHODS::

METHOD:: eq
The eq shown. The display, the analyser, the meters, the header and the cards all
follow it; nil leaves an empty panel.

METHOD:: view
The panel.

METHOD:: eqView
The link::Classes/ParaEQView:: in the middle.

METHOD:: spectrum
The link::Classes/ParaEQSpectrum:: behind the curve.

METHOD:: meter
The FxMeter at the display's right.

METHOD:: header
The FxHeader along the top: power, name, presets, save, the code::...:: menu.

METHOD:: running
Whether the analysis runs and the eq's node says its levels. A window sets it while
the panel is showing; false switches both off.

METHOD:: hints
A link::Classes/FxHintBar:: to label every control. The display writes to it itself.

METHOD:: subject
What the hints call the thing being shaped.

METHOD:: selected
The handle being worked on - one of the five bands, or one of the two cuts.

METHOD:: shownBand
The band the band card shows, 0 to 4. Selecting a cut leaves it where it was.

METHOD:: widgets
Every control the panel draws, for a check or a picture: the spectrum switch, the
range menu, the band selector, the cuts' switches, freqs, slopes and res, then the
five bands' freqs, gains and widths.

METHOD:: isClosed

METHOD:: refresh

METHOD:: remove
Stops listening to the eq, frees the analyser and switches the meter off. In the
window's onClose.
